In this lesson we'll look at how to pick a sample that actually represents the population you care about. The big idea is this: if your sample leans one way, your conclusions will lean one way too. That's bias. There are a few common ways to gather data. Simple random sampling means everyone in the population has an equal shot at being picked. Stratified sampling splits people into groups, like age brackets, then randomly samples within each group so proportions are kept. Cluster sampling picks whole groups at random, like whole schools. Convenience sampling just grabs whoever's easiest, and voluntary response means people opt in themselves. Both of those last two are usually biased, so watch out. Bias can also sneak in when people don't respond, when questions are worded in a leading way, or when researchers quietly favour results that match what they hoped for. On the ethics side, studies should have informed consent, anonymity, the right to withdraw, and no harm to participants. And a trustworthy report should tell you the sample size, how people were chosen, the response rate, and any limits. If that's missing, be suspicious. Now let's see it in action.